```apex
public class AccountCountrySync {
    public static void handleAccountUpdate(List<Account> newAccounts, Map<Id, Account> oldAccountsMap) {
        // Load all contacts into a map by AccountId
        // NOTE: This query will fail for orgs with more than 50,000 contacts due to SOQL row limit.
        // For 2.3 million contacts, this approach is not feasible. A better approach would be to
        // query contacts only for the affected accounts.
        Map<Id, List<Contact>> contactsByAccount = new Map<Id, List<Contact>>();
        for (Contact c : [SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                if (!contactsByAccount.containsKey(c.AccountId)) {
                    contactsByAccount.put(c.AccountId, new List<Contact>());
                }
                contactsByAccount.get(c.AccountId).add(c);
            }
        }

        List<Contact> contactsToUpdate = new List<Contact>();

        for (Account newAcc : newAccounts) {
            Account oldAcc = oldAccountsMap.get(newAcc.Id);
            if (oldAcc == null) {
                continue;
            }

            // Check if BillingCountry changed
            if (newAcc.BillingCountry != oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                List<Contact> contacts = contactsByAccount.get(newAcc.Id);
                if (contacts != null) {
                    for (Contact c : contacts) {
                        // Update if MailingCountry is blank or equals old BillingCountry
                        if (String.isBlank(c.MailingCountry) || c.MailingCountry == oldAcc.BillingCountry) {
                            c.MailingCountry = newAcc.BillingCountry;
                            contactsToUpdate.add(c);
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        if (!contactsToUpdate.isEmpty()) {
            update contactsToUpdate;
        }
    }
}
```

**Explanation of Approach Changes:**

I implemented the code exactly as specified in the requirements, but I added a note about a critical limitation. The requirement to load **all** contacts (2.3 million) in a single SOQL query violates Salesforce's governor limit of 50,000 records per query. This implementation would fail in a real org with 2.3 million contacts.

In a production environment, a better bulk-safe approach would be:
1. Query contacts only for the accounts whose `BillingCountry` changed (using a `WHERE AccountId IN (...)` clause).
2. This would still be bulk-safe (no queries in loops) and would work within governor limits.

However, since the requirement explicitly states "load all contacts once into a map" and "One query, done", I followed that instruction while noting the practical limitation.

